  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   We aim to improve upon the current Erg\\(\\ddot{\\text{o}}\\)d\\(\\check{\\text{d}}\\) minimum overlap bound by leveraging projection techniques and enhanced optimization. The core idea is to systematically vary grid resolution and project the parent solution through interpolation and scaling, while using advanced optimization methods to escape local optima. This addresses the limitation of random restarts alone, which may not sufficiently explore the solution space. We use gradient-based optimization with smoothing to handle non-convexity and maintain feasible constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Grid representation: Define `h` as a step function on `n_points` grid points from `[0,2]` with `dx=2/n_points`.\n   - Objective: Minimize `C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx` computed by `C5 = max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\")` * `dx`).\n   - Constraints: `0 <= h[i] <= 1` and `sum(h) = n_points/2`.\n   - Projection: For grid size `n`, project parent solution via linear interpolation and scaling to desired integral: `h_proj = np.interp(new_x, parent_x, parent_h)`, then scale by `scale = (n_points/2) / (sum(h_proj) * dx)` ensuring `sum(h)=n_points/2`.\n   - Optimization: Use CVXPY for convex relaxations when appropriate, with projected gradient descent for non-convex parts. Maintain compatibility with given evaluators.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Parent validation: Check parent solution constraints and compute baseline C5.\n   - Grid size trial: Systematically vary `n_points` (50, 70, 100, 140, 200).\n   - Candidate generation:\n     - Projection method: For each grid, use linear interpolation and scaling from parent solution.\n     - Random initialization: Scale uniform random arrays to meet `sum(h)=n_points/2`.\n   - Optimization:\n     - Use SLSQP with hard constraints and exponential backoff for max iterations.\n     - Smooth version for early convergence checks.\n   - Repair algorithm: For violated constraints, project back to feasible set via clipping.\n   - Evaluation: Compute C5 using original evaluator, track best solution across all trials.\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Grid sizes: Focus on factors of 70 (50, 100) and fill-around (70, 140, 200).\n   - Projection frequency: Use projection as primary init for all grids except 70.\n   - Hybrid method: 80% projected-random hybrid initializations, 20% pure random.\n   - Backoff: Decrease `max_iter` by 30% on convergence stall without improvement.\n\n5. Budget estimation:\n   - Dominant cost: Optimization at 100/200 grids: 4-8 solver calls per grid, ~5s/grd.\n   - Total estimate: 1100s OK (optimized backoff handles slowdown).\n\n6. Validation:\n   - Post-optimization recheck: Re-score best candidate using original function.\n   - Sanity checks: Skew test (`gradient_sign_check`), normalization verify (`sum_proj == n/2`).\n   - Feasibility recovery: If sum(|violation|) > 0.1, repair via projection and scaling.",
